    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/11/new-balance-1080-v15-review-2026.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The new Infinion midsole feels moderately soft and highly protective rather than mushy, with a smooth heel strike from the large bevel/lateral groove and a cushioned but moderately flexible forefoot feel.",
        "Responsiveness: Ride is moderately responsive with clear bounce and resiliency (better than recent versions), handling daily pace changes and fartleks but lacking true snappy pop for all-out fast efforts.",
        "Stability: It is still a neutral shoe but gains mild guidance from a wide non-narrowing midfoot, straight last, medial support material, and heel sidewalls; the stiff 2/3-height heel counter helps rearfoot control, while overall flexibility means only moderate torsional rigidity and some upper-related lateral sliding in turns.",
        "Durability: After about 40 miles, outsole wear is minimal (slight lateral midfoot wear, no outsole peeling), and expected lifespan is average to slightly above average, though exposed midsole suggests best durability on roads over trails."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/11/new-balance-1080-v15-multi-tester.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The 39.9/33.9 mm platform and soft supercritical Infinion midsole deliver cushy impact protection, but the firm rear outsole and 6 mm drop can feel less smooth at slower paces.",
        "Responsiveness: Infinion is described as lighter, bouncier, and more energetic than prior Fresh Foam, and the major weight drop shifts the shoe toward faster, more performance-oriented daily running.",
        "Stability: Lockdown is strong from the secure upper and rigid heel counter, with firm rear rubber adding rearfoot control; however, the narrow forefoot, lateral sidewall carve-out, and limited midfoot/arch outsole coverage may reduce lateral stability for some runners.",
        "Durability: The 1080 line is characterized as historically long lasting, and the new foam is claimed to be similarly durable; no clear outsole wear issues were reported in this text, though forefoot grip was noted as below average on some surfaces."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/new-balance-1080-v15-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The Infinion midsole is described as soft and squishy with plenty of underfoot protection and smooth transitions, with 2 mm more foam than v14 (though one tester felt it was a bit firmer).",
        "Responsiveness: Testers reported better rebound and a touch more responsiveness than v14, with bounce/pop that stays lively deeper into longer runs.",
        "Stability: The ride is neutral, and the redesigned upper (double-layer mesh + gusseted tongue) improves foot hold/lockdown; the narrower fit may help narrow feet but reduce comfort for wider feet.",
        "Durability: Durability feedback was positive, with comments that Infinion is intended to maintain cushioning longer and that outsole coverage includes a tacky forefoot and a durable heel."
      ]
    }
